# RSVPs and invites are stored in Supabase in production — see
# RSVPS-AND-REMINDERS-SETUP.md and INVITES-SETUP.md. SUPABASE_URL and
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY are NOT set here: they're Vercel dashboard-only
# environment variables (Project → Settings → Environment Variables),
# unneeded for `npm run dev`, which stays on the local flat-file store
# (server/index.js). Never prefix them with VITE_ — that would bundle the
# service-role key (full read/write access) into the public JavaScript.
#
# Brevo transactional email — order-confirmation emails (sent right after a
# paid invite is saved, from api/invites/index.js and server/index.js's
# POST /api/invites) use BREVO_API_KEY + EMAIL_FROM directly from
# process.env — see src/utils/email.js. Get an API key from Brevo →
# Settings → SMTP & API → API Keys. EMAIL_FROM's address (or its domain)
# must be a verified sender in the Brevo dashboard, or sends will
# fail/bounce. Never prefix either with VITE_.
# BREVO_API_KEY=xkeysib-XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX
# EMAIL_FROM=InviteMyPeople <hello@invitemypeople.com>
#
# The SAME two variable names (BREVO_API_KEY, EMAIL_FROM) are ALSO needed,
# set SEPARATELY, for the 4-hour-before-event reminder email — that one is
# sent by the Supabase Edge Function (supabase/functions/send-event-
# reminders), which can't see this .env file or Vercel's dashboard vars, so
# it needs its own copies via `supabase secrets set BREVO_API_KEY=...
# EMAIL_FROM="..."`. This feature is the first in this repo where the same
# variable name has to be configured in more than one secret store — see
# RSVPS-AND-REMINDERS-SETUP.md for the full three-places picture.

# Razorpay Standard Checkout — required for the Invitation Builder's payment
# step (see src/components/builder/PaymentModal.jsx). Get test keys from
# the Razorpay Dashboard → Settings → API Keys. RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET is
# server-only — never prefix it with VITE_.
# RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=rzp_test_XXXXXXXXXXXXXX
# RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX
# VITE_RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=rzp_test_XXXXXXXXXXXXXX

# Spotify Web API (Client Credentials flow) — powers the Background Music
# Selection search in the Invitation Builder (see
# src/components/builder/SongSearchPicker.jsx, src/utils/spotify.js). Free,
# app-only auth — no user login, no paid Spotify plan needed. Get both from
# the Spotify Developer Dashboard (https://developer.spotify.com/dashboard)
# → Create App (any name/redirect URI — Client Credentials never uses the
# redirect URI) → Settings. Both are server-only — read directly from
# process.env by src/utils/spotify.js — never prefix either with VITE_, or
# the secret would ship in the public JS bundle. See MUSIC-SETUP.md.
# SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX
# SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET=XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX

# B2B Partner Portal — see B2B-PARTNER-PORTAL-SETUP.md. Unlike RSVPs/invites,
# these routes need a real Supabase project even for `npm run dev` (there's
# no flat-file fallback for a login system), so SUPABASE_URL/
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY above must be set locally too if you're working
# on this feature — run the SQL in supabase/migrations/0001_b2b_partner_
# portal.sql against that project first.
#
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL/VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY are the browser-side Supabase Auth
# client (src/utils/supabaseClient.js) — the anon key is meant to be public,
# unlike SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY, so VITE_-prefixing it here is correct.
# Get both from Supabase Dashboard → Project Settings → API.
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://xxxxxxxxxxxx.supabase.co
# VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX
#
# ADMIN_EMAILS is the allowlist for /admin/partners (see api/_lib/auth.js) —
# a comma-separated list of Supabase Auth emails treated as admins. No
# separate admin-accounts table; anyone who can log in with one of these
# emails via the normal partner/admin login can approve or reject partners.
# Server-only — never prefix with VITE_, or anyone could read the allowlist
# out of the public bundle (though not use it, since it's checked against a
# verified session server-side either way — still, don't expose it).
# ADMIN_EMAILS=founder@invitemypeople.com, ops@invitemypeople.com
